1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:06,925
Can you show me git add?

2
00:00:07,467 --> 00:00:12,704
git add copies changes from the working directory into the staging area:

3
00:00:12,704 --> 00:00:14,886
the draft of your next commit.

4
00:00:14,886 --> 00:00:17,650
We compare git add FILE with git add .

5
00:00:17,650 --> 00:00:18,741
and git add -A.

6
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:24,899
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

7
00:00:25,433 --> 00:00:26,877
Three changes:

8
00:00:26,877 --> 00:00:29,248
Stage exactly one file:

9
00:00:29,800 --> 00:00:31,871
Stage exactly one file:

10
00:00:31,871 --> 00:00:37,634
menu.txt moved to the first column (staged); the others did not.

11
00:00:37,634 --> 00:00:39,704
Now everything at once:

12
00:00:40,267 --> 00:00:45,462
menu.txt moved to the first column (staged); the others did not.

13
00:00:45,462 --> 00:00:47,329
Now everything at once:

14
00:00:47,329 --> 00:00:48,060
git add .

15
00:00:48,060 --> 00:00:52,281
stages every change in the current folder and below:

16
00:00:52,281 --> 00:00:56,989
modified and new files (and deletions too, since Git 2.0).

17
00:00:56,989 --> 00:01:03,321
git add -A does the same for the whole repository, whatever folder you are in.

18
00:01:05,067 --> 00:01:06,598
Let me try something.

19
00:01:06,598 --> 00:01:12,941
Stage a file that should never be committed (a local secret) with a careless git add .:

20
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:14,014
A .env:

21
00:01:14,014 --> 00:01:15,996
the secrets file is staged.

22
00:01:15,996 --> 00:01:17,757
It is not committed yet:

23
00:01:17,757 --> 00:01:23,702
the staging area is a draft, and you can take things out of it before the commit.

24
00:01:23,702 --> 00:01:28,180
That is the moment to look at git status before every commit.

25
00:01:29,933 --> 00:01:38,228
Unstage it (lesson 30 covers git restore --staged), and make Git ignore it from now on (lesson 73):

26
00:01:39,967 --> 00:01:40,680
Got it.

27
00:01:40,680 --> 00:01:43,431
What do I need to remember?

28
00:01:43,967 --> 00:01:48,601
git add copies the current content of a file into the staging area.

29
00:01:49,167 --> 00:01:49,263
.

30
00:01:49,263 --> 00:01:57,991
= this folder and below; -A = whole repository; -u = tracked files only; -p = hunk by hunk.

31
00:01:58,533 --> 00:02:00,884
Staged content is a snapshot:

32
00:02:00,884 --> 00:02:03,558
later edits need another git add.

33
00:02:05,300 --> 00:02:08,576
I keep hearing about the staging area.

34
00:02:08,576 --> 00:02:10,215
What is it, really?

35
00:02:10,767 --> 00:02:14,576
Why Git has a staging area between your files and your commits,

36
00:02:14,576 --> 00:02:18,506
and how to see what is in it with git diff and git diff --staged.

37
00:02:20,267 --> 00:02:24,666
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

38
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:27,059
Two unrelated changes:

39
00:02:27,059 --> 00:02:30,862
a typo fix in the README and a new menu item.

40
00:02:30,862 --> 00:02:33,060
Stage only the README fix.

41
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:35,714
Stage only the README fix.

42
00:02:35,714 --> 00:02:37,584
Now ask both questions:

43
00:02:37,584 --> 00:02:42,625
Commit the staged part only, then the rest as a second commit:

44
00:02:43,167 --> 00:02:47,421
Commit the staged part only, then the rest as a second commit:

45
00:02:47,421 --> 00:02:50,508
Two focused commits from one working session.

46
00:02:50,508 --> 00:02:54,556
Each can be reviewed, reverted or cherry-picked on its own.

47
00:02:56,300 --> 00:02:57,868
Let me try something.

48
00:02:57,868 --> 00:03:02,274
Commit and expect everything to be in it, without checking:

49
00:03:02,833 --> 00:03:07,809
The message says "and add chai" but git status still shows M menu.txt:

50
00:03:07,809 --> 00:03:12,144
chai was never staged, so the commit contains only the price.

51
00:03:12,144 --> 00:03:15,343
The commit message now lies about the commit.

52
00:03:17,100 --> 00:03:20,601
The commit is not pushed, so it may be changed:

53
00:03:20,601 --> 00:03:25,144
stage the missing part and amend the last commit (lesson 11):

54
00:03:26,900 --> 00:03:27,613
Got it.

55
00:03:27,613 --> 00:03:30,365
What do I need to remember?

56
00:03:30,900 --> 00:03:34,260
The staging area is the draft of the next commit.

57
00:03:34,800 --> 00:03:41,875
git diff = not staged; git diff --staged = will be committed; git diff HEAD = everything.

58
00:03:42,433 --> 00:03:48,133
Stage deliberately to keep commits focused; check git diff --staged before committing.

59
00:03:49,867 --> 00:03:52,086
How does git commit work?

60
00:03:52,633 --> 00:03:59,308
How to record a commit, how to write a good commit message, and how to fix the last commit with --amend.

61
00:04:01,067 --> 00:04:05,466
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

62
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:07,219
[main 1a2b3c4]:

63
00:04:07,219 --> 00:04:13,149
the branch and the new commit's short ID; then a summary of what changed.

64
00:04:13,149 --> 00:04:15,180
Look at the whole commit:

65
00:04:15,733 --> 00:04:16,899
[main 1a2b3c4]:

66
00:04:16,899 --> 00:04:22,572
the branch and the new commit's short ID; then a summary of what changed.

67
00:04:22,572 --> 00:04:24,515
Look at the whole commit:

68
00:04:24,515 --> 00:04:27,313
-m twice gives a summary and a body.

69
00:04:27,867 --> 00:04:31,044
-m twice gives a summary and a body.

70
00:04:31,044 --> 00:04:37,751
Without -m, Git opens your editor (core.editor, or $EDITOR) for the message.

71
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:41,340
Let me try something.

72
00:04:41,340 --> 00:04:45,195
A typo in the message, and a forgotten file:

73
00:04:45,733 --> 00:04:51,987
The summary has a typo and holidays.txt belongs to the same change but is still untracked.

74
00:04:51,987 --> 00:04:57,823
The commit has not been pushed (no remote here at all), so it is safe to rewrite it.

75
00:04:59,567 --> 00:05:01,759
--amend replaced the commit:

76
00:05:01,759 --> 00:05:05,438
same parent, new snapshot, new message, new ID.

77
00:05:05,438 --> 00:05:09,195
Never amend a commit that others already pulled:

78
00:05:09,195 --> 00:05:15,771
their copy still has the old one (lesson 32 shows the safe alternative, git revert).

79
00:05:17,533 --> 00:05:18,247
Got it.

80
00:05:18,247 --> 00:05:20,998
What do I need to remember?

81
00:05:21,533 --> 00:05:27,753
git commit records the staging area as a snapshot with a message, author, date and parent.

82
00:05:28,300 --> 00:05:29,005
Message:

83
00:05:29,005 --> 00:05:34,554
short imperative summary, blank line, a body that explains why.

84
00:05:35,100 --> 00:05:40,149
--amend rewrites the last commit; only for commits nobody else has yet.

85
00:05:41,900 --> 00:05:44,644
What actually happens during a commit?

86
00:05:45,200 --> 00:05:47,106
What a commit is made of:

87
00:05:47,106 --> 00:05:55,111
a snapshot of all files (not a diff), plus an ID, an author, a timestamp and a link to its parent commit.

88
00:05:55,111 --> 00:05:57,779
Those parent links are the history.

89
00:05:59,533 --> 00:06:03,933
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

90
00:06:04,467 --> 00:06:07,997
The raw commit object of the latest commit:

91
00:06:07,997 --> 00:06:08,900
Four parts:

92
00:06:08,900 --> 00:06:13,006
tree (the snapshot), parent (the previous commit),

93
00:06:13,006 --> 00:06:19,082
author/committer (name, e-mail, Unix time and time zone), and the message.

94
00:06:19,082 --> 00:06:23,351
The tree lists every file, not just the changed one:

95
00:06:23,900 --> 00:06:24,778
Four parts:

96
00:06:24,778 --> 00:06:28,768
tree (the snapshot), parent (the previous commit),

97
00:06:28,768 --> 00:06:34,673
author/committer (name, e-mail, Unix time and time zone), and the message.

98
00:06:34,673 --> 00:06:38,823
The tree lists every file, not just the changed one:

99
00:06:38,823 --> 00:06:45,367
README.md and menu.txt did not change in this commit, yet the snapshot lists them:

100
00:06:45,367 --> 00:06:52,788
Git stores complete snapshots and reuses unchanged files (the same blob ID as in the parent),

101
00:06:52,788 --> 00:06:54,224
so it stays small.

102
00:06:54,767 --> 00:07:00,640
README.md and menu.txt did not change in this commit, yet the snapshot lists them:

103
00:07:00,640 --> 00:07:07,301
Git stores complete snapshots and reuses unchanged files (the same blob ID as in the parent),

104
00:07:07,301 --> 00:07:08,590
so it stays small.

105
00:07:08,590 --> 00:07:14,391
The diffs you see in git show are computed on the fly by comparing two snapshots.

106
00:07:16,133 --> 00:07:17,584
Let me try something.

107
00:07:17,584 --> 00:07:23,178
Try to change an old commit's message "in place" by editing the history directly.

108
00:07:23,733 --> 00:07:26,520
Why can't an old commit simply be edited?

109
00:07:26,520 --> 00:07:30,462
Its ID is a hash of its content including its parent's ID.

110
00:07:30,462 --> 00:07:36,580
Changing a commit changes its ID, which changes the parent recorded in every later commit,

111
00:07:36,580 --> 00:07:38,483
which changes all their IDs.

112
00:07:40,233 --> 00:07:48,553
Rewriting older commits is possible, as a deliberate new history, with interactive rebase (lesson 63).

113
00:07:48,553 --> 00:07:55,078
For a mistake in the latest, unpushed commit, --amend (above) is the right tool.

114
00:07:56,833 --> 00:07:57,547
Got it.

115
00:07:57,547 --> 00:08:00,298
What do I need to remember?

116
00:08:00,833 --> 00:08:10,833
A commit = snapshot (tree) + parent(s) + author/committer + message; its ID is a hash of all of it.

117
00:08:11,367 --> 00:08:17,481
Git stores snapshots and reuses unchanged files; diffs are computed when you ask.

118
00:08:18,033 --> 00:08:22,882
Changing a commit changes its ID and the IDs of everything after it:

119
00:08:22,882 --> 00:08:24,593
history is a hash chain.
